WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.260
Model provenance and checkpoint trust are about making model use evidence-based and repeatable.

2
00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:13.480
A checkpoint should not enter shared or production workflows simply because it is convenient, popular, or already present on a machine.

3
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.980
The team should know where it came from, what changed, and why it is approved.

4
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:28.100
Document origin, lineage, intended use, limitations, licenses, approvals, operational status, and retirement expectations.

5
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:35.080
Treat checkpoint formats, conversions, companion files, and loading paths as security-relevant engineering decisions.

6
00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:42.060
Preserve integrity evidence with hashes, signatures, attestations, registries, and controlled storage where appropriate.

7
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.580
Evaluate behavior before deployment.

8
00:00:44.680 --> 00:00:56.360
Provenance can identify the artifact, but task fitness requires testing against intended use, constraints, prompt sets, performance expectations, and known failure modes.

9
00:00:56.460 --> 00:01:00.540
Keep the results tied to the model version so future changes can be compared.

10
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.220
Finally, operate the model lifecycle.

11
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:14.600
Pin approved versions, separate experiments from production, monitor runtime context, maintain inventory, prepare rollback paths, and revoke artifacts when trust changes.

12
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:21.020
Sustainable checkpoint trust comes from lifecycle discipline, not from a single download or one-time review.

13
00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:30.180
The practical standard is simple: a team should be able to explain the model, verify the file, justify the approval, observe the deployment, and reverse the change.

14
00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:37.160
When those habits are routine, checkpoint trust becomes an engineering process instead of a fragile assumption.
